[# Conformance gate policy — the cx-wide gate REGISTER (derived).

   DEFAULT = enforced (deny-by-default, mirroring spec/core/security.md's grant
   model): a failing fixture BLOCKS its gate. A suite marked `advisory` is the
   spec-first frontier — its failures are RUN and REPORTED but do NOT block the
   gate. Promote `advisory -> enforced` as the implementation lands; that
   promotion is the gate-progression control.

   WHERE A SUITE'S STATUS LIVES (D49a, #1633; RULED: RS-27). On the suite's own
   element: `[test-suite name=sync ring=2 gate=advisory reason='…']` (gate= is
   enforced | advisory; advisory carries a reason=). The status travels with
   the file into whatever repository carries it, and the grading core
   (vcx/corpus — the fixture shards and `cx corpus`) reads it there. The
   [module] rows below are DERIVED from those elements: a row states the
   element's gate= (an absent row or an absent element gate= is enforced) and
   an advisory row copies the element's reason= byte for byte.
   `make gates-manifest-gate` refuses a row that disagrees with its suite's
   element (naming both), an advisory element with no row, and an element
   gate= the grader refuses (scripts/gates_register_check.cx; its corpus is
   conformance/gates_register.cxd). To change a suite's status, edit its
   element and copy the change here; the element is the source.

   Resolution order for a fixture's effective gate (most specific wins):
     per-case `gate=` attr  >  the suite element's `gate=`  >  per-suite default here  >  enforced

   Per-case overrides live on the [case] itself (gate=advisory|pending|skip).
   The per-suite default= rows (code, stdlib, packages, xpath-31-parity) are
   read by the graders directly. See conformance/stdlib/AUTHORING.md and
   conformance/stdlib/SPEC-FINDINGS.md. #]

[gate-policy default=enforced

  [# core program + format suites: fully enforced (no frontier carve-out — the
     code.txt frontier is driven to zero by the spec-compliance effort). #]
  [suite name=code default=enforced]

  [# stdlib: implemented modules are enforced; not-yet-implemented modules are
     advisory (their fixtures are the spec-first worklist — red until built,
     must not block). Flip a module to enforced the moment it lands. #]
  [suite name=stdlib default=enforced
    [# implemented + enforced: bytes env format hash path random strings time uuid
       (left at the suite default; listed here for the reader) #]
    [module name=bytes   gate=enforced]
    [module name=env     gate=enforced reason='ENFORCED, all cases green; env-038 unblocked — operator-elements now propagate err-value operands uniformly with calls (§9.2), so [= [$env:vars] [$env:vars]] surfaces CXER0271 instead of comparing the two errs (SPEC-FINDINGS AK)']
    [module name=format  gate=enforced]
    [module name=hash    gate=enforced]
    [module name=path    gate=enforced]
    [module name=random  gate=enforced]
    [module name=strings gate=enforced]
    [module name=time    gate=enforced reason='full §3 impl complete + spec-faithful (ENFORCED, all cases green); §3.10 recurrence amendment landed — RFC 5545 RRULE (parse/format, BYDAY ordinals, BYSETPOS, all BY* filter/expand per §3.3.10) + 5/6-field cron + @macros, DST-aware wall-clock-local expansion, max-occurrences budget (CXER3325) and CXER3320-3326 band wired, all pure (no clock); occurrences cross-validated against a hand-rolled RFC 5545 reference for last-weekday-of-month / DST-spanning weekly / biweekly MO,WE,FR (time-117..147)']
    [module name=uuid    gate=enforced]

    [# not yet implemented -> advisory (spec-first frontier) #]
    [module name=graphql gate=enforced reason='IMPLEMENTED — the Ring 1 PURE GraphQL CLIENT codec (spec/03-approved/stdlib/graphql.md; #1429, RULED: GQL-1, 1430-c, 1430-g) lands with stdlib/graphql.cx and vcx/code/stdlib_graphql.v, so the 49 cases the corpus carried BEFORE the code are graded here. The corpus is hermetic and every one of the twelve public defs is Ring 1 and PURE — a document parsed and emitted, a request envelope built from declared variables, a response envelope read, its errors classified by extensions.code, an operation validated against a schema from SDL or from an introspection result, a document costed against a bound, and a connection page projected — so no case carries a grant=, a port, a store URL or --manual-clock, and none reaches cx-platform/http: the transport is the connector kit\'s kind=graphql adapter (connector.md §3.11) and the server half is a recorded trigger (GQL-1). graphql-001..008 are the document, 010..016 the variables and the request envelope, 020..028 the response envelope and the error classification a 200 hides, 030..036 the schema and validation, 040..047 the Relay connection the kit registers as its pagination style, and 050..059 the EIGHT ATTACK CLASSES of the spec §5. Twelve codes are allocated, CXER6500..CXER6511 inside the band governance.md §9.6 registers for this module']
    [module name=sync gate=advisory reason='SPEC-FIRST FRONTIER — incremental sync (#1434, #728 component 3; RULED: 1434-a and 1085-e) is specced (spec/03-approved/platform/sync.md, status=new) and UNIMPLEMENTED: there is no stdlib/sync.cx and no vcx/platform/stdlib_sync.v, so all 132 cases are RED by construction and that red list IS the implementation list (the audit #1422 and connector #1430 precedent). The corpus is hermetic — thirteen of the modules twenty verbs are Ring 1 and pure, and EVERY decision the contract makes lives in that half (the diff, the advance, the dedup key, the horizon prune, the reseed plan, the status, the record shapes) — so no case carries a grant=, a port, a store URL or --manual-clock; the Ring 2 half is present as its CXER0271 denial lane only and the granted round trips against an in-tree mock and a mem:// store live in vcx/tests/sync_live_test.v. Flip to enforced in the commit that lands the code']
    [module name=soap gate=enforced reason='IMPLEMENTED — the Ring 1 PURE SOAP codec (spec/03-approved/stdlib/soap.md; #1456, RULED: 1430-c, 1430-d, 1430-g, 1456-a) lands with stdlib/soap.cx and vcx/code/stdlib_soap.v, so the 66 cases the corpus carried BEFORE the code are graded here. The corpus is hermetic and every one of the sixteen public defs is Ring 1 and PURE — an envelope built and read in ONE representation for both versions, one canonical reading of the two fault vocabularies, the WS-Addressing header, WS-Security\'s four members, the DETACHED signature over the Body and the WSDL the kit\'s ingest reads — so no case carries a grant=, a port, a store URL or --manual-clock, and none reaches cx-platform/http: the transport including TLS is the connector kit\'s kind=soap adapter (connector.md §3.12) and the server half is a landing. Time enters as an ARGUMENT and so does entropy. soap-001..014 are the FOURTEEN ATTACK CLASSES of the spec §5.1 and come first, 020..029 the envelope, 030..039 the fault, 040..045 WS-Addressing, 050..059 WS-Security, 060..067 the signature over the Body and 070..077 the WSDL. Sixteen codes are allocated, CXER6600..CXER6615 inside the band governance.md §9.6 registers for this module']
    [module name=connector gate=enforced reason='cx-platform/connector — the connector kit (spec/03-approved/platform/connector.md §10; #1430, RULED: 728-CK-1..728-CK-10 and 1085-e). ENFORCED as of the commit that landed stdlib/connector.cx + vcx/platform/stdlib_connector.v: the corpus was written BEFORE the module on purpose (the audit #1422 and mailbox #1413 precedent, RULED: S-2) and that red list WAS the implementation worklist, so the row flips here together with connector.md status=new -> current and the cx-stdlib module count. Hermetic — twelve of the modules eighteen verbs are Ring 1 and pure, and EVERY decision the kit makes lives in that half (the walk, the classification of a response, the budget window, the signing, the declaration checker, the OpenAPI transform) — so no case carries a grant=, a port or --manual-clock; $now is always an argument, which is what makes a Retry-After of "Sun, 06 Nov 1994 08:49:37 GMT" grade identically in 2026 and in 2046. The Ring 2 half is present as its CXER0271 denial lane only (the #1028 lesson: a deny case never reaches the shape it names) and the granted round trips against the in-tree mock [?http-service] live in vcx/tests/connector_live_test.v. The eight attack classes of §8 — auth-header injection, credential smuggling, pagination loops and cursor cycles, Retry-After abuse, rate-limit window edges, schema bombs, host escape, ingestion hostility — are graded here. The last twenty-five cases (connector-200..224) are the ADAPTER CONFORMANCE HARNESS of connector.md §3.9/§10.1 (RULED: 1430-c, 1430-d) — the cases every kind adapter must pass, written against the mock adapter of §10.1 because a core invariant that holds for http and fails for a database is not an invariant. All twenty-five are ENFORCED as of the commit that landed the kind seam and the mock adapter (#1480, RULED: 1430-d): the twelve hooks of §3.9.4 are one dispatch point each, the pagination-style registry, the classification table, the audit detail vocabulary and the gateway schema fragment are the ADAPTER declarations read through the kind table, http re-registers through it with no behaviour change, and kind=mock is declared by the kit\'s own CX beside the release adapters (RULED: RS-25; the compile-time test-build gate that registered it in the TEST build alone retired with it), so every profile that carries the kit grades the core through an adapter that is not http. Two of the twenty-five were CORRECTED against a spec sentence rather than the engine bent to them: connector-209 and connector-210 wrote retry-after= as a seconds string where §3.3 makes it the attribute [?rate-limit] emits on CXER0151 and code.md §10.2.5 makes that a DURATION (the enforced connector-029 already pins 1m for the same max=0 computation), and connector-212 passed a bare [err] as a call argument where code.md §9.2 propagates it out of the call, so it now uses the [outcome [err]] carrier its twin connector-087 uses. THE kind=db SECTION (#1455; RULED: 1430-c, 1455-a, 1430-d, 1430-g) is the second transport\'s corpus and every one of its forty-three cases carries its OWN gate=advisory: there is no vcx/platform/stdlib_connector_db.v and no db row in the kind table on this tree, so kind=db answers CXER6326 today and the cases are RED by construction — that red list IS the adapter\'s implementation worklist, the connector-001..134 and flow-080..086 precedent. connector-300..324 are the §10.1 adapter conformance harness INSTANTIATED for kind=db, aligned last-two-digits with connector-200..224 row for row so a reviewer comparing two adapters compares two aligned lists; connector-330..337 are the kind\'s own attack classes of connector.md §3.8.8 (SQL in a cursor value staying a bind parameter, a keyset that never advances, a page over the row bound, a DSN in a gateway row, a key over the cursor length bound, a statement outside the bound schema, CR/LF in a key value, an information-schema read over the node bound); connector-340..349 are the cursor-COLUMN walk and the kind\'s own vocabulary against the in-tree sqlite fixture the corpus states once and never restates. They flip to enforced in the commit that lands the adapter\'s V file and registers db in the kind table. THE kind=webhook SECTION (#1460; RULED: 1430-e, 1430-d, 1430-g) is the third transport\'s corpus and the FIRST INBOUND one, and every one of its forty-three cases is ENFORCED as of the commit that registered webhook in the kind table and added its arms at the twelve dispatch points of §3.9.4: the band was written first and that red list WAS the adapter\'s implementation worklist. What is new is the direction — the vendor drives this transport, the deployment\'s [?http-service] mounts the gateway\'s declared route, and the core is unchanged by that: the walk is the inbound QUEUE, the §3.4 window is read in reverse (the sender spends it and the door answers a 429 with Retry-After), the credential chain is the same three refusals and the audit record is one per delivery with [outbound] still the head. connector-400..424 are the §10.1 adapter conformance harness INSTANTIATED for kind=webhook, aligned last-two-digits with connector-200..224 row for row and column for column with kind=db\'s 300..324; connector-430..437 are the kind\'s own attack classes of connector.md §3.10.8 (a forged signature, a replayed delivery, a body over the bound, an undeclared route, a sender flooding the window, a delivery outside the replay window, a verification key in a gateway row, CR/LF in a delivery id); connector-440..449 are the inbound landing on the connector\'s journal stream, the back-pressure, the event-catalog discovery, the declaration refusals and the AsyncAPI ingest. Every expectation is derived from one in-tree delivery fixture the corpus states once — two deliveries with their headers, their real HMAC-SHA256 signatures over a fixed key and their store content addresses, and a third carrying a well-formed MAC made with a rotated key. Two codes are added with this section, CXER6328 E_CONNECTOR_SIGNATURE_INVALID and CXER6329 E_CONNECTOR_REPLAY_WINDOW, both inside the band governance.md §9.6 already registers. Every case in the band is PURE — eight of the twelve hooks are, and the walk, the classification, the confinement, the record, the discovery parse and the ingest all live in that half — so the whole band is enforced rather than a subset, and the door\'s own round trip against a real [?http-service] is vcx/tests/connector_webhook_test.v. connector-406 inverted to name kind=vsam, a kind that still has no adapter, the way connector-206 and connector-306 do. THE kind=graphql SECTION (#1429; RULED: GQL-1, 1430-c, 1430-d, 1430-g) is the fourth transport\'s corpus and the FIRST whose failures arrive inside a 200: every one of its forty-three cases carries its OWN gate=advisory because there is no vcx/platform/stdlib_connector_graphql.v, no graphql row in the kind table and no vcx/code/stdlib_graphql.v for the codec beneath it, so kind=graphql answers CXER6326 today and the cases are RED by construction. What is new is that no status code says what happened: a GraphQL endpoint answers 200 and puts the failure in the body, and it may answer 200 with half the data and an error for the rest — which is why §3.9.5 gives classify-of an opaque [outcome] and not a status, and why this section adds ONE code, CXER6330 E_CONNECTOR_PARTIAL_RESULT, the partial page the kit refuses rather than yields. The bytes are the codec\'s (cx-stdlib/graphql, #1429) and its refusals CXER6500..CXER6511 are RE-RAISED unchanged rather than re-coded in this band, the sync precedent — nothing is graded twice, and the codec\'s own cases are conformance/stdlib/graphql.cxd. connector-500..524 are the §10.1 adapter conformance harness INSTANTIATED for kind=graphql, aligned last-two-digits with connector-200..224 row for row and column for column with kind=db\'s 300..324 and kind=webhook\'s 400..424; connector-530..537 are the kind\'s own attack classes of connector.md §3.11.8 (a depth bomb, alias amplification, a batched request, a partial page, an introspection bomb, a cursor over the length bound, a bearer in a gateway row, CR/LF in a cursor); connector-540..549 are the connection walk whose continuation is an opaque endCursor, the classification a 200 hides, the record that carries the operation name and never the query text, discovery by introspection that may honestly be empty, the declaration refusals and the SDL ingest of 1430-g. Every expectation is derived from one in-tree endpoint the corpus states once — the same catalog schema, operation and two pages conformance/stdlib/graphql.cxd states for the codec, so a reader comparing the two files compares one system. They flip to enforced in the commit that lands the adapter\'s V file and registers graphql in the kind table.THE kind=soap SECTION (#1456; RULED: 1430-c, 1430-d, 1430-g) is the fifth transport\'s corpus and the one where the STATUS LINE LIES IN THE OTHER DIRECTION: every one of its forty-three cases carries its OWN gate=advisory because there is no vcx/platform/stdlib_connector_soap.v, no soap row in the kind table and no vcx/code/stdlib_soap.v for the codec beneath it, so kind=soap answers CXER6326 today and the cases are RED by construction. The SOAP 1.1 HTTP binding mandates a 500 for EVERY fault, so the core\'s 5xx-retryable row would retry a permanent soap:Client fault until the attempt bound and spend the tenant\'s whole budget on a request that cannot succeed — a 500 carrying a well-formed Fault is therefore classified by the FAULT and never by the 500 (connector-635), which is §3.9.5\'s "classify-of receives an opaque [outcome] and not a status" read from the opposite side to kind=graphql\'s. This section adds NO code to the band: every refusal is either the core\'s or the codec\'s, and SOAP admits no half-answer so CXER6330 has no row in this kind\'s table. The bytes are the codec\'s (cx-stdlib/soap, #1456) and its refusals CXER6600..CXER6615 are RE-RAISED unchanged, the sync precedent — nothing is graded twice, and the codec\'s own cases are conformance/stdlib/soap.cxd. connector-600..624 are the §10.1 adapter conformance harness INSTANTIATED for kind=soap, aligned last-two-digits with connector-200..224 row for row and column for column with kind=db\'s 300..324, kind=webhook\'s 400..424 and kind=graphql\'s 500..524; connector-630..637 are the kind\'s own attack classes of connector.md §3.12.8 (a signature that does not cover the Body, a wsu:Id that occurs twice, a replayed envelope outside its timestamp window, an external entity in a RESPONSE, a mustUnderstand header nobody declared, a Fault behind a 500 that would otherwise be retried forever, a WS-Security password in a gateway row, CR/LF in a body-carried cursor); connector-640..649 are the cursor-in-the-BODY walk that makes §2.2\'s short word `cursor` mean the right thing for this kind, the classification the 500 hides, the record that carries the action and the version and never the envelope or the token, discovery by WSDL that may honestly be empty, the declaration refusals and the WSDL ingest of 1430-g. Every expectation is derived from one in-tree order service the corpus states once — the same service, envelopes, faults and WSDL conformance/stdlib/soap.cxd states for the codec, so a reader comparing the two files compares one system. They flip to enforced in the commit that lands the adapter\'s V file and registers soap in the kind table.THE kind=stream SECTION (#1461; RULED: 1430-e, 1430-d, 1430-g, INT-19) is the eighth transport\'s corpus and the FIRST whose CONNECTION OUTLIVES THE CALL: every one of its forty-three cases carries its OWN gate=advisory because there is no vcx/platform/stdlib_connector_stream.v, no stream row in the kind table, no §3.7 upgrade surface in cx-platform/http and no cx-stdlib/ws beneath either, so kind=stream answers CXER6326 today and the cases are RED by construction. What is new is that every other kind ASKS and is answered while this one is TOLD: a gateway opens one connection — a WebSocket over http.md §3.7 or an SSE response over §3.6 — and the vendor pushes down it, so the walk has no page parameter and no page number, its pagination style IS the resume token (a cursor that never pages backward, which is why a replayed token is :cycle), its bounds are a DURATION, a BYTE BUDGET and a reconnect count rather than an item count (§15 row 41 predicted exactly this), and liveness is declared because silence on a socket is indistinguishable from health — a missed heartbeat is the retryable failure the whole heartbeat= declaration exists for. This section adds NO code to the band, the kind=soap precedent: every refusal is the core\'s, the ws codec\'s CXER6900..CXER6911 or http\'s CXER4552..CXER4561, and the last two are RE-RAISED unchanged — nothing is graded twice, the codec\'s own cases are conformance/stdlib/ws.cxd and the upgrade\'s are conformance/platform/http.cxd. connector-900..924 are the §10.1 adapter conformance harness INSTANTIATED for kind=stream, aligned last-two-digits with connector-200..224 row for row and column for column with kind=db\'s 300..324, kind=webhook\'s 400..424, kind=graphql\'s 500..524, kind=soap\'s 600..624, kind=sftp\'s 700..724 and kind=ftp\'s 800..824; connector-930..937 are the kind\'s own attack classes of connector.md §3.15.8, the four #1461 named with the decision first (a frame over the bound, a resume token from another tenant, a server that never heartbeats, a redirect to a third host) and then a token that pages backward, a credential in the SUBSCRIPTION MESSAGE, CR/LF in a resume token and a reconnect storm; connector-940..949 are the resume-token walk, the two classification tables a stream is read by (a close code and an upgrade status), the record that carries the protocol and the cursor and never an event body, discovery by an AsyncAPI document read through kind=webhook\'s own reader, and the AsyncAPI ingest whose four TODOs are the resume location, the heartbeat, the subscription message and idempotency. Every expectation is derived from one in-tree feed the corpus states once — one gateway, two drains, one heartbeat and one close. They flip to enforced in the commit that lands the adapter\'s V file and registers stream in the kind table.THE kind=imap AND kind=smtp SECTIONS (#1462; RULED: 1430-e, 1430-d, 1430-g) are the ninth and tenth transports\' corpora and are the mail row of 1430-e landing as the TWO adapters a mailbox and a submission server actually are — CX hosts no mailbox (1085-e) and these are connectors to someone else\'s. Every one of their eighty-six cases carries its OWN gate=advisory because there is no vcx/platform/stdlib_connector_imap.v, no vcx/platform/stdlib_connector_smtp.v, no imap or smtp row in the kind table and no vcx/platform/stdlib_imap.v or stdlib_smtp.v beneath either, so both kinds answer CXER6326 today and the cases are RED by construction. What is new in kind=imap is a SOURCE that can declare its own cursor space void: a page is a UID RANGE addressed by (UIDVALIDITY, UID) and never by a sequence number, which renumbers on every EXPUNGE; the resume point across runs is the MODSEQ where the server has CONDSTORE and the UIDNEXT where it does not, the degraded path imap.md §3.4 names and the record carries as degraded=true; and a UIDVALIDITY change mid-walk is a REFUSAL and not a fifth stop reason — the cursor is void, the walk reseeds and never continues, and the code is imap\'s own CXER5813 re-raised, which sync.md §3.3 already states reaching that contract as CXER6405. What is new in kind=smtp is the first SINK: a verb IS a delivery, so the style is delivery, the continuation is always absent and the walk is ONE page with a second message being a second call ordered by flow; the classification is the protocol\'s own 4xx-retryable / 5xx-not split; and the wait is a DECLARED defer-wait= answered on the two replies that mean later, a 421 and a 450, because SMTP carries no Retry-After and §3.3\'s normalization and 5-minute clamp are the core\'s over whatever number the adapter answers — the kind=stream heartbeat= precedent for a fact a protocol states nowhere. Both sections add NO code to the band, the kind=soap and kind=stream precedent: every refusal is the core\'s, imap\'s CXER5800..CXER5815 or smtp\'s CXER5700..CXER5714, and the last two are RE-RAISED unchanged — nothing is graded twice, and the modules\' own cases are conformance/platform/imap.cxd and conformance/platform/smtp.cxd. connector-1000..1024 and connector-1100..1124 are the §10.1 adapter conformance harness INSTANTIATED for the two kinds, aligned last-two-digits with connector-200..224 row for row and column for column with kind=db\'s 300..324, kind=webhook\'s 400..424, kind=graphql\'s 500..524, kind=soap\'s 600..624, kind=sftp\'s 700..724, kind=ftp\'s 800..824 and kind=stream\'s 900..924 — 950..999 cannot host an aligned harness, which is why these two kinds take the next aligned hundred-blocks; connector-1030..1037 are kind=imap\'s own attack classes of connector.md §3.16.8, the three #1462 named with the decision first (a UIDVALIDITY change mid-walk, a message over the bound, a resume point that goes backward) and then a credential in a gateway row, CR/LF in a mailbox name, a resume point from another tenant, a page over the item bound and a folder list over the discovery bound; connector-1130..1137 are kind=smtp\'s own of §3.17.8, #1462\'s three first (a header injection in a recipient, a message over the bound, a recipient list over the bound) and then a credential in a gateway row, a 421 declaring an hour, an envelope sender the gateway never declared, a relay to a third host and a bare LF in the body; connector-1040..1049 and connector-1140..1149 are the two kinds\' own vocabulary — the UID-range walk and its degraded fallback, the one-page delivery walk, the two classification tables, the records that carry the MAILBOX and the RECIPIENT DOMAIN and never a message body, a local part or a Subject header, the envelope that is never derived from the headers, the landings on journal, discovery by folder list and by EHLO answer, the declaration refusals, and the two ingests whose input describes NO OPERATIONS — a mailbox has folders and an EHLO answer states capabilities, which is 1430-g step 9\'s named case for a third and a fourth kind. Every expectation is derived from one in-tree mailbox and one in-tree submission the corpus states once. They flip to enforced in the commits that land the adapters\' V files and register imap and smtp in the kind table.THE kind=bus SECTION (#1463; RULED: 1430-e, 1430-d, 1430-g) is the eleventh transport\'s corpus and the FIRST whose page ARRIVES rather than being fetched and must then be ACKNOWLEDGED: every one of its forty-three cases carries its OWN gate=advisory because there is no vcx/platform/stdlib_connector_bus.v and no bus row in the kind table on this tree, so kind=bus answers CXER6326 today and the cases are RED by construction. What is new is that the position exists TWICE — the walk\'s continuation is the stream SEQUENCE (JetStream\'s today, the journal stream\'s own seq= on an embedded fabric, which is the same number because a durable fabric stream IS a journal stream) and the group\'s store-persisted cumulative offset is the resume point above it, with the CONSUMER GROUP as the identity that makes a sequence mean anything and ack= as the declaration saying how far apart the two may drift (on-process redelivers, on-receive loses the batch, none is the transient plane and claiming complete-ordered over it is CXER6321). The attack this kind is written against is the POISON MESSAGE: under a cumulative ack one message that fails its fold on every delivery blocks a group\'s tail forever and retrying it is the redelivery storm, so the answer is fabric.md §9.1\'s dead-letter policy DECLARED on the gateway — max-deliveries= and dlq= together or not at all, CXER4931 re-raised — and the message is dead-lettered and COUNTED rather than retried. cx-stdlib/bus is UNTOUCHED (fabric.md §8, N-BUS-1): the name kind=bus is 1430-e\'s word for the transport and not a second mode on that module, which keeps its synchronous ordered in-process contract unchanged. This section adds NO code to the band, the kind=soap, kind=stream, kind=imap and kind=smtp precedent: every refusal is the core\'s or fabric\'s CXER4920..CXER4949 and CXER4990/CXER4991 re-raised unchanged — nothing is graded twice, and fabric\'s own cases are conformance/platform/fabric.cxd. connector-1200..1224 are the §10.1 adapter conformance harness INSTANTIATED for kind=bus, aligned last-two-digits with connector-200..224 row for row and column for column with kind=db\'s 300..324, kind=webhook\'s 400..424, kind=graphql\'s 500..524, kind=soap\'s 600..624, kind=sftp\'s 700..724, kind=ftp\'s 800..824, kind=stream\'s 900..924, kind=imap\'s 1000..1024 and kind=smtp\'s 1100..1124; connector-1230..1237 are the kind\'s own attack classes of connector.md §3.18.8, the four #1463 named with the decision first (a poison message, a redelivery storm, a subject the feature did not declare, an ack for a sequence never received) and then a credential in a gateway row, CR/LF in a subject, a message over the bound and stream info over the discovery bound; connector-1240..1249 are the sequence walk that never goes backward, the group as the resume identity, the three ack modes, the fabric classification, publish as the verb and its landing on journal, the record that carries the SUBJECT and never a payload, discovery by stream and consumer info that may honestly be empty, the declaration refusals and the AsyncAPI ingest whose four TODOs are the group, the ack mode, the dead-letter policy and idempotency — read through the same reader kind=webhook and kind=stream already use. Every expectation is derived from one in-tree stream the corpus states once. They flip to enforced in the commit that lands the adapter\'s V file and registers bus in the kind table.']
    [module name=crypto   gate=enforced reason='full §3 impl complete + spec-faithful (ENFORCED, all cases green); crypto-015/028 fixtures corrected to the spec [?fallback PRIMARY [recover-with SECONDARY]] form (§10.2.4; the 2-arg shorthand is not grammar, SPEC-FINDINGS AD); 18 capability-denial cases enforced (random cap-guard on the 4 entropy generators, SPEC-FINDINGS M/N); §3.8/§3.10 JWT amendment landed — rsa-verify (RSASSA-PKCS1-v1_5) + ecdsa-verify (P-256 JOSE raw r‖s) over math.big + fail-closed jwt-verify/jwks-parse/claim/jwk-by-kid, cross-validated against OpenSSL vectors (crypto-062..088); jwks-fetch net-gated (live transport pending like net/http, denial case enforced)']
